            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Portuguese for to debate, then the Portuguese for the argument."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: argumentar",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "argumentar — \"to argue (with reasons), to make a case\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ela argumentou que o bairro precisa de mais árvores. She argued that the neighbourhood needs more trees."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Argumentar que… — to argue that… It is about giving reasons, not about quarrelling."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "argumentar is a learned borrowing from Latin argūmentārī \"to adduce proof of a thing\" (EtymDB, Lewis & Short), formed on argūmentum \"proof\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
